diff --git a/docs/service-supervision.md b/docs/service-supervision.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..fd3973e0b6aa462a11392a5f2108ee085819d47c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/service-supervision.md
@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+# Dashboard service supervision
+
+The candidate in `deploy/systemd/` runs the collector, snapshot writer and API
+as three independent system services. It adds no application or consensus code.
+The local rehearsal uses Ubuntu's systemd 255 and Linux Node 24.21.0. Validate
+the selected host and installed versions again before public deployment.
+
+## Provision the host
+
+Keep a reviewed, read-only release at `/opt/fsn-stats/current` and the reviewed
+Node executable at `/opt/fsn-stats/node/bin/node`. The working directory contains
+the installed root backend dependencies. The frontend build and TLS proxy remain
+separate. Only the deployment administrator may change the release or runtime.
+
+Each unit uses `DynamicUser=yes`: systemd assigns a separate unprivileged identity
+and removes it after shutdown. Do not grant these identities ownership of the
+release or persistent files. The processes use loopback TCP for the collector
+and PostgreSQL, not Unix peer authentication. No service writes application files.
+The units make the filesystem read-only, hide home directories, isolate temporary
+files/devices, remove capabilities and disable core dumps.
+
+Prepare root-owned, mode 0600 environment files at:
+
+- `/etc/fsn-stats/collector.env`
+- `/etc/fsn-stats/writer.env`
+- `/etc/fsn-stats/api.env`
+
+Use systemd environment-file syntax (`NAME=value`, no `export` or shell expansion).
+Supply the explicit settings in [deployment configuration](deployment-configuration.md),
+[snapshot storage](snapshot-storage.md) and [dashboard profile](dashboard-profile.md).
+Choose `GEOIP_MODE=disabled` or the complete checked [MMDB profile](geoip-mmdb.md).
+The local supervision drill's sampling and timeout values are test settings;
+the public manifest must reconcile them with freshness and stop deadlines.
+
+The environment files must not contain `WS_CREDENTIALS_FILE`, `DB_PASSWORD_FILE`,
+`WS_SECRET`, `SQLHOST` or `SQLPASS`. The units supply the two supported credential
+paths themselves. Store the source secrets in a root-owned mode 0700 directory
+`/etc/fsn-stats/credentials`, with mode 0600 files:
+
+| File | Recipient | Contents |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `telemetry.json` | Collector | Per-node telemetry credential roster |
+| `writer.password` | Writer | Restricted database writer password |
+| `api.password` | API | Read-only database password |
+
+`LoadCredential=` supplies a private copy to the corresponding service. Passwords
+are not command-line arguments or environment values. Keep the source files
+outside the release. Changing a source credential requires restarting its
+recipient; database role/password rotation still needs coordinated provisioning.
+The dashboard never receives validator signing keys.
+
+Provision PostgreSQL, the versioned schema and separate writer/read-only roles
+using [snapshot storage](snapshot-storage.md). Manage PostgreSQL through the
+selected distribution's own service. These units neither install nor initialize it.
+
+Install the three service files into `/etc/systemd/system/` and the journal
+configuration into `/etc/systemd/journald@fsn-stats.conf`. Run `systemd-analyze verify`
+on the installed unit files, then `systemctl daemon-reload`. Verify permissions,
+paths, credentials, schema and the chosen profile before enabling the three units
+for boot. There is no automatic installer or public activation in this change.
+
+## Start, inspect and recover
+
+Start the collector, writer and API with `systemctl start fsn-stats-collector
+fsn-stats-writer fsn-stats-api`. Each runs the Node entry point directly, without
+an npm or shell wrapper. There are no dependencies that stop healthy components
+when a neighbour restarts. The writer retries collector/database loss and the
+API returns unavailable when its database or fresh snapshot is missing.
+
+`Type=exec` confirms execution, not readiness. Verify the loopback API, complete
+fresh snapshots, per-node receipts and the public browser/proxy separately.
+`systemctl is-active` alone is insufficient. This configuration does not detect
+a live but hung process; monitoring and notification remain deployment work.
+
+`Restart=always` restarts unexpected exits after five seconds, including a clean
+unexpected exit. `systemctl stop` deliberately leaves a unit stopped. At most five
+starts within sixty seconds are allowed; repeated configuration failures leave
+the service failed instead of retrying indefinitely. Inspect both:
+
+```sh
+systemctl status fsn-stats-collector fsn-stats-writer fsn-stats-api
+journalctl -u fsn-stats-collector -u fsn-stats-writer -u fsn-stats-api
+journalctl --namespace=fsn-stats
+```
+
+Service-manager lifecycle messages are in the host journal; application output
+is in the dashboard namespace. After fixing a repeated startup failure, use
+`systemctl reset-failed` for that unit and explicitly start it. Restarts use the
+currently selected release/configuration; they do not choose a rollback version.
+Keep and verify the previous reviewed release before changing the selection.
+
+For a controlled full shutdown, stop the writer first, then the collector and
+API. The writer stops sampling, drains its pending transaction and closes its
+pool. The collector/API use Node's normal SIGTERM termination; in-flight HTTP
+requests and reporter sockets may be interrupted. Collector observations are
+rebuilt on reconnect; the database retains the last committed snapshot. The
+supervisor stops remaining processes after fifteen seconds. Choose database
+timeouts below that deadline and rehearse the actual profile under load.
+
+## Journal lifecycle and scope
+
+The supplied namespace configuration uses persistent storage with a 64 MiB
+budget and 8 MiB files, a 16 MiB/2 MiB volatile fallback, seven-day maximum
+retention and no forwarding to syslog, console, kernel or wall. It requests
+1 GiB/64 MiB free-space reserves for persistent/volatile storage respectively.
+These limits apply to this namespace, not the entire host. Journald rotation
+removes archived files; active files and allocation can exceed a nominal budget.
+Size limits can remove entries before seven days. Retention is not an archive.
+
+The configured 1000-message/30-second rate limit can suppress diagnostics, and
+journald adjusts its effective burst with available disk space. Review suppression
+messages and normal/outage log rates. Do not interpret silence as service health.
+Changing journal configuration requires restarting that namespace's journal
+service; coordinate application restarts and verify new messages afterwards.
+
+The local drill checks the actual service processes, crash recovery, a database
+outage, pending-write shutdown, stale API behaviour, intentional stop, repeated
+startup failure and repair. Its rotation exercise uses the same 16 MiB volatile
+budget, disables compression, and disables rate limiting in a separate temporary
+namespace containing only a synthetic log generator. It does not prove retention across reboot,
+public-host capacity or production rate-limiter behaviour.
+
+The existing nginx acceptance template still writes file logs. Its supervision,
+log rotation/reopening, public certificates/renewal, host reboot, health alerts
+and the common production manifest remain separate acceptance work. No service
+is enabled persistently by the local drill.
+
+References: [systemd service semantics](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/v255/man/systemd.service.xml),
+[execution and credentials](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/v255/man/systemd.exec.xml),
+[journal configuration](https://github.com/systemd/systemd/blob/v255/man/journald.conf.xml).
